These are the commands you can use when demonstrating your environment to your instructor.
Step 1: Activate the environment
Open Ubuntu Terminal:
cd ~/cn-tcp-lab
source .venv/bin/activate

Check Open vSwitch:
sudo systemctl status openvswitch-switch --no-pager

Expected:
Active: active (exited)

Step 2: Show your installed environment
Copy and run:
uname -a
mn --version
ovs-vsctl --version
iperf3 --version
python --version
sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_available_congestion_control
sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control

Your congestion-control algorithms should include:
reno cubic bbr

Your default algorithm can remain cubic.
Step 3: Demonstrate Mininet
Run:
sudo mn -c
sudo mn --switch ovsbr --controller none --test pingall
sudo mn -c

Expected:
*** Results: 0% dropped (2/2 received)

Step 4: Demonstrate Reno, CUBIC and BBR
Open Terminal 1:
iperf3 -s

Keep the server running.

Open Terminal 2:
iperf3 -c 127.0.0.1 -t 2 -C reno
iperf3 -c 127.0.0.1 -t 2 -C cubic
iperf3 -c 127.0.0.1 -t 2 -C bbr

All three tests should complete successfully.

Stop the server in Terminal 1 using Ctrl + C.

Note: If an iperf3 server is already running, reuse it rather than starting another one.
Step 5: Demonstrate packet capture
Open Terminal 1:
iperf3 -s

Open Terminal 2:
cd ~/cn-tcp-lab
dumpcap -i lo -a duration:10 -w captures/demo.pcapng

While capturing, open Terminal 3:
iperf3 -c 127.0.0.1 -t 5

After the capture finishes, verify:
capinfos captures/demo.pcapng

Open the capture:
wireshark captures/demo.pcapng

Inside Wireshark, enter this display filter:
tcp

You should see the TCP packets generated by iperf3.

Stop the server afterward.
Step 6: Clean up
Run:
sudo mn -c
deactivate
